# .github-guard — declarations github-guard reads from the default branch on
# GitHub. See antimatter-studios/agent-skills, skill github-guard.
# git-config format: check with `git config -f .github-guard --list`, and
# double-quote any value containing # or ;.

# [checks] required — what branch protection demands before main takes a merge.
#
#   ci-ok   ci.yml: the one always-run gate. It `needs:` every other job in
#           that workflow and fails when any of them failed, was cancelled or
#           was SKIPPED — the `test` matrix (`cargo build`, `cargo test` and
#           `clippy --locked --all-targets -- -D warnings` on ubuntu-latest
#           and macos-latest, each against the six sibling crates cloned at
#           their pinned tags), and `fmt` (`cargo fmt --check`, once, because
#           it is the same answer on every platform).
#
# WHY ONE NAME AND NOT THREE (#18). This file used to declare `fmt`,
# `test / ubuntu-latest` and `test / macos-latest` — the real check names, and
# the note here said so and asked for this replacement. Two of those three are
# legs of ONE matrix, and a leg's check name is derived from its `os` value, so
# the list drifts the moment the matrix does. #15 is a `clippy -D warnings`
# failure on aarch64 Linux that neither current target can see; fixing it means
# an `ubuntu-24.04-arm` leg, which would report `test / ubuntu-24.04-arm` on
# every pull request and gate nothing at all, because nobody would have edited
# this file in the same breath. That is the failure the #197 sweep found in five
# repositories in this constellation.
#
# The opposite direction is worse and has also happened here. A required context
# that no job produces reads to GitHub as permanently PENDING, not failing: with
# `enforce_admins` on, nothing merges, and there is no red check and no log to
# point at — only a merge button that never lights up. Renaming the `test` job,
# or dropping a leg, produces that state silently.
#
# `needs: [test, fmt]` names the matrix JOB, not its legs, so both directions
# close at once: the gate waits on a new leg the moment it exists, and there is
# no pinned leg name left to rot.
#
# AND NOTHING ELSE, which is where this diverges from what rust-img-vhdx#105
# first proposed and what rust-lzo1x#14 did — both kept the real names declared
# BESIDE the aggregate. That keeps exactly the drift the aggregate was added to
# remove, because the leg names are still pinned. rust-fs-xfs#207 settled it the
# other way and is the reference this repository follows.
#
# tests/ci_aggregate_gate.rs holds both halves to it mechanically: every job in
# ci.yml has to be in `ci-ok`'s `needs`, `ci-ok` has to carry `if: always()`,
# and this file has to require `ci-ok` and nothing else. Without that test the
# aggregate can quietly stop watching a job and still report green, which is a
# worse gate than the pinned list it replaced.
#
# THE NAME DOES NOT MOVE WHEN THE JOBS DO, which is why this needs no ordering
# dance on the CI side: github-guard reads this file from the DEFAULT BRANCH, so
# a pull request that renames a gating job can never be the one whose protection
# learns the new name. Requiring the aggregate means the jobs behind it can be
# renamed, split or replaced wholesale in a single pull request that protection
# never notices.
#
# Only checks that run on `pull_request` may gate, and here that is free:
# ci.yml is the ONLY workflow in this repository and it carries a bare
# `pull_request:` trigger, so there is no release-on-tag workflow and no nightly
# cron to require by accident. (The sibling fs crates have both, and a required
# check that cannot report on a pull request blocks every merge forever.)
#
# The name must match the check-run name GitHub reports, which is the job's
# `name:` rather than its key — `ci-ok` for both.
#
# Third-party reviewers need no mention: github-protect-main.sh filters
# `select(.app.slug=="github-actions")` on every query it makes, so nothing
# reporting under another app can enter the required set through this file.
[checks]
	required = ci-ok
